From c0d102760da627de61c155e145debd593a5dea8c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 13:12:49 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(multiaxis): a swarf raster line rises off the blade on a
 rapid under its lying pose

The base raster leaves every line on a feed straight up Z whose end is
in air, so the stamp stated it upright: a 90 deg turn off the lying pose
on every raster end, taken through an air link and turned back on the
next plunge. stamp_rise::rapid_rise now runs that rise as a rapid holding
the pose the line cut with, when the rise is straight up Z, ends in air,
never brings the ball nearer the part than it started, and the held
lean (not under level) has its flank proved in air at every half-radius
sample. Anything unproved keeps the upright feed and the link law.

A rapid claims no contact, so the verifier's posed-contact law (which
convicted #17's posed feed in air) has nothing to read; the posed-rapid
solid law judges it like every link retract. Both app blade tests and
their full-band twins verify clean; the pivot5d envelope scan passes.

Air links on the debug blade: swarf 34 -> 23, ladder 53 -> 34 (full
band 55 -> 44, 86 -> 67); the ratchets in swarf_pose_continuity are
lowered to those counts (main was red on the ladder's pair, 53 > 51 and
86 > 84).

Fixes #48

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
